🧠 Since demuxlet already removed most doublets (only same-patient doublets remain), I'll use a light-touch approach later. First, let's run detect_doublets — note the actual 10x runs correspond to `condition` (stim pooled in one run, ctrl in another), not `donor` (donors were pooled within each run).
🔧 detect_doublets(batch_key=condition)
   → is_bimodal, batch_key, score_distribution, histogram, candidate_thresholds, recommended_threshold

🧠 The distribution isn't bimodal, and since demuxlet already removed most cross-patient doublets (only same-patient doublets remain, which Scrublet can't distinguish well from real cells), the median+3*MAD threshold (flagging 10.71% of cells) would be too aggressive and cut real cells from the upper tail. Following the light-touch guidance, I'll use the higher of the two per-batch Scrublet automatic thresholds (stim: 0.648), applied dataset-wide as the more conservative cutoff.
🔧 filter_doublets(threshold=0.6482692445451002)
   → threshold, before_cells, after_cells, removed, pct_removed, checkpoint

🧠 Only 4 cells removed, consistent with the fact that most doublets were already removed upstream by demuxlet. Now normalize.
🔧 normalize()
   → target_sum, n_top_genes_requested, n_hvgs_flagged, hvg_batch_key, raw_counts_stashed, checkpoint

🧠 The dataset has 8 donors (candidate batch key) and condition is confounded with the 10x run. IFN-β stimulation is known to cause strong transcriptional shifts (ISG induction) that could split cell types into separate per-condition clusters if uncorrected. To align cell types across conditions for consistent annotation and composition comparison, I'll use scVI with batch_key combining donor and condition (one batch per sample), which corrects the embedding while leaving raw counts untouched for DE/composition analysis.
